cut pineapples he puts it on the  nightstand next to his bed and it makes  you wonder if that's going to be  important later on while she was getting  her facial rachel did work up the nerve  and she tells him she thinks their  marriage was a mistake he turns things  around saying oh wow you hid yourself  really well here i was thinking you were  this cool chick and really you're just a  baby she fires back with actual examples  of him being a baby on their trip how  he's cuddled by his mother how she  showed up for their honeymoon and the  never-ending tantrum that he's been  throwing over the room this exchange  does show the faulty foundation of their  relationship he can't hear her i really  even understand where she's coming from  for him everything's good therefore the  only options are her going along with  that or messing it up when she says that  she doesn't want to spend her life as a  plus one his answer is just don't be  that and he can't understand why that's  a problem the mossbachers are heading  out for their scuba dive armin stops  them to let them know that the thief was  caught and that they'll be getting their  jewelry back they ask if it was an  employee and when he says yes olivia  makes a point of asking for the name and  they do find out it was kai  armond throws it out there that he can't  believe it that he was such a nice guy  he was such a good employee he never  caused any problems and he would have  never guessed and so paula realizes that  she corrupted him  mark and quinn have their dive and it  looks amazing they even swim with the  sea tortoise and there's several points  in this finale where they emphasized the  natural beauty of the island i suppose  they did that throughout the series but  it really stands out here and it feels a  little different knowing that the one  indigenous person we met is probably on  their way to prison and while the guys  are in the water we see paula take the  necklace she got from kai and throw it  overboard at the desk garmin gets a call  from the owner he knows he's going to  get fired now he heads to his office he  tells dylan to come with him inside he  starts crushing up pills says they're  gonna go hard and he's basically off to  the races  when dylan asks him if it's a kamikaze  situation if maybe he's gonna be taken  down too armand just says hey they  exploit me i exploit you that's how it  works and if you're gonna lose your job  you might as well do it now before you  waste your entire life  when they return to the room olivia  confronts paula telling her that she  knows she was involved with the robbery  paula eventually comes clean and just  says you know you don't understand it  was never about me the conversation ends  with olivia saying you know something  bad could have happened my mom could  have been hurt apologist says the truth  something bad did happen over at the spa  tanya comes in to tell belinda goodbye  she thanks her for the help thanks her  for being so great but essentially says  she doesn't want to do the business  anymore she turns it around saying that  it's an unhealthy situation for her  because it's like falling into this old  pattern she latches on to someone and  then she tries to control them with her  money the truth is she really is just  focused on greg now things are going  good there he seems to accept her so  that gives her some room to have this  insight and it comes down to her doing  something that makes her feel good  something that works for her rather than  focusing on something that could help  someone else  armond is all tuned up and he goes to  host his last dinner you see him in all  his glory working the crowd seating  everyone you can really see how good  he's gotten at his job over the years of  course he does look a little tweaked out  and shane notices that when he mentions  it that opens the door for him and  rachel to have a conversation about  what's going on he tells his wife that  whenever she gets over whatever's going  on with her he'll be there he's her  husband and he cares and then he  proceeds to get angry when she doesn't  magically get better as a result of him  saying that and he even bangs on the  table asking her why are you trying to  ruin everything at one point she tells  him that she feels weirdly alone when  they're together and when he doesn't  really have anything to say to that he  just says fine then be alone and he goes  to the bar and starts throwing back  double tequilas greg has another  coughing fit at the bar and he can see  that tanya is concerned so he tells her  that he has some health issues he says  don't be surprised if i drop dead and  tonya drops one of her best replies in a  series where she's had quite a few of  them saying that death is the one fully  immersive experience she hasn't tried  yet and with that everything's in place  for the ending  the end plays out like this armand is  pumped saying he did an amazing job at  dinner he continues to get high with  dylan and a couple of the other staff  members that have now joined him and  eventually he gets up to walk to the  pineapple suite and gets a great idea to  take a dump in shane's suitcase  belinda throws her business plan in the  trash knowing that fat envelope of cash  is the only thing she's ever going to  see from tonya she had given her number  to rachel earlier when she saw her  crying and she does call belinda helps  her get her own room and then listens to  her talk about her problems but when  rachel asks her for advice she says  she's all out she doesn't have anything  left in the mossbacher room paula's in  bed crying and olivia gets closer to hug  her make her feel better she says she  [ __ ] everything up and olivia says  it's okay  shane comes back to his room armond's  still in there he tries to hide in the  closet but shane smells something and he  discovers the present that armon left  him in his suitcase he calls the front  desk to complain armond tries to sneak  out shane hears a noise and he grabs the  knife and then eventually stabs him when  he comes around the corner and it does  turn out to be fatal after it all plays  out you see shane talking to the police  with the hotel staff by his side he  doesn't look terribly torn up and it's  wild because they seem to buy his story  of self-defense without really delaying  him in any way like he stabbed someone  to death in his room one day and he's  leaving the jurisdiction the next  quinn wakes up on the beach for his last  morning you can see a tear roll down his  cheek as he takes in all the beauty  tanya walks into the frame and she goes  to the water to scatter her mother's  ashes she's finally ready to put it all  behind her and looks almost as blissful  as she did when she was imagining the  moment earlier in the season at the  airport armond's remains are being  loaded onto the flight to honolulu  shane's watching through the window and  it brings us full circle to the teaser  flash forward from the first episode  only this time we get to see what  happens after rachel does arrive at the  ticket counter after spending her night  alone and he walks directly over and she  looks at him and says everything's fine  i'm happy i promise i will be happy and  they hug and she just sort of stares  into the distance and he just sort of  looks relieved that she's finally come  to her senses  at the gate we get a final look at all  the guests greg and tanya look happy  nicole and mark are beaming you can see  that she's got her 75 000 bracelets back  paula and olivia are reading it's time  to go back to the real world they start  boarding with quinn lagging behind his  family he stops waits till they make it  through the gate and then he makes a  break for it back at the resort you see  the staff they're smiling and waving to  welcome the next boat full of vips and  in the closing shot you see quinn in the  canoe paddling towards the idyllic  sunrise  if you're keeping score how did  everybody do well the rich people they  all got what they wanted more or less  and the people trying to make a living  serving them not so much and you might  think to yourself well that sucks but as  a piece of social satire it's pretty  consistent with where things were going  and with reality  you may not like it but don't be like  the mossbachers and try to cling onto  the idea that it's not how things really  are money rules everything money makes  money the rich get richer and you could  go on but i think it really would have  dulled the edge of what the show was  trying to say if it didn't end this way  [ __ ] rolls downhill and the consequences  for making mistakes is different  depending on where you rank in the  pecking order there's a real possibility  that the characters in this show and the  people in society that they're a  reflection of would prefer to be  miserable rather than give up anything  that they have and feel like they  deserve you could say what you want  about the system that makes all this  possible its sustainability the damage  that it continues to cause across the  globe just to keep itself going but i  think what the show was really good at  getting across is the feeling that these  people have about their situation as  opposed to what their situation really  is  mark lays it out perfectly when he says  nobody seeds their privilege that's  absurd it goes against human nature  we're all just trying to win the game of  life i think that's what you can take  away from the ending these aren't the  worst people in the world they aren't  the most diabolical characters ever  conceived for a television series and  you can probably even find some moments  of relatability in some of their  perspectives they've bought into the  system and even when they can't see it  and maybe it doesn't even happen  consciously but they're trying to hold  on to that and preserve it if you look  at the individual endings the robbery  fixed things up for nicole and mark the  situation with armond and the ripple  effects of belinda not having anything  left when rachel really did need advice  that all brought shane and rachel back  together at first i wasn't sure about  the quinn leaving the airport and  paddling off into the sunrise that whole  thing but i suppose it's grown on me for  one he was the only guest who was  actually getting something from being on  the island and things could have felt  really bleak if there wasn't at least an  uplifting moment there what's funny is  that when you first watch it you're  thinking oh well maybe he gets a happy  ending but if they showed us anything  through mark and nicole and how he slept  on the beach and how olivia's so cynical  towards them it's that they don't really  pay much attention to their kids so it's  actually possible that they just didn't  notice he wasn't on the plane when they  went to honolulu and then when they got  there they went after him there's really  no guarantee that he's gonna get to stay  there thanks to her treatments with  belinda tonya makes what she thinks is a  healthy decision to avoid falling into a  pattern and she does that by falling  into a more immediately instantly  gratifying pattern and the idea that  rachel finds herself in this position so  fast after marrying into money it  actually means that she's got something  to lose and that changes things in a  really uncomfortable way status is a  hell of a drug olivia and paula had the  one relationship and were the characters  that you could kind of still hope that  they would evolve given their age i mean  it's easy to be critical of your family  and their place in things when they're  still supporting you through college  olivia doesn't seem like someone who's  taking on debt to be at school but what  comes next you know what about when she  integrates into that same system paula  made a mistake and someone else paid the  price there's no undoing that she did it  thinking it wasn't about her but it  wasn't really ever about kai either it  was to hurt the mossbachers and she felt  justified because of olivia stealing her  previous boyfriend and you have to hope  that she'll be able to see that and  become a better person and of course  that goes for all of them but with the  younger people it just feels like it  might be more possible armond and kai's  endings are obviously tragic and they  hit different things in different ways  neither one of them was perfect in the  first episode armond didn't even realize  that the woman he was training was  pregnant and even though he was coaxed  into it by paula i did make a decision  to go into that room and try to steal  the bracelets you could say that armand  should have just backed down but he's a  character who's worked in this business  for a long time he knows that once shane  owns him there'll be no end to what he  has to deal with over the next week so  you kind of understand why he decided to  fight back i think the point is though  that there are consequences for their  mistakes whereas you look at a guy like  shane and you could make a debate kind  of argument about how he was right all  along but all of it really would be  missing the point what he did was  self-defense someone came into his room  and from his perspective that was a  threatening situation but look how  easily he walked away from it a guy like  kai he's gonna spend time in prison the  crime he committed breaking and entering  the value of what he stole his life is  never gonna be the same so i thought it  was a fun six episodes and a really nice  commentary on these characters there  were a lot of smart moments in the way  they were presented and i thought it was  one of the better uses of the flash  forward sort of in medius rest openings  that have become really popular because  streaming shows really want to get you  hooked in right at the beginning you get  the idea that it's more of a character  study slash social satire than a mystery  but you also know someone's gonna end up  dead and that makes a lot of little  things take on weight quinn and mark's  diving and his canoe trips greg's  coughing armon's declaration that he's  gonna go hard with the drugs and of  course shane's grabbing the knife i  think they pulled that off really well i  also quite liked how they kept the focus  away from the tragedy at the end kai's  ordeal all happens off screen there's no  direct mention of arm and after he dies  life at the resort goes on as if they  never existed the guests lives go on  after a great vacation with just a few  minor inconveniences the performances  were all superb the natural backdrop was  great to look at if you can just sort of  not focus on the imperialism of it all  and the score was absolutely amazing i  can't really overstate how many times i  found myself locked into whatever was  happening on screen  and realized that i was nearly  hypnotized by the music and i suppose  that's as good a place as any to leave  things i could go on but let's do that  in the comments instead  let me know what you thought about the  show the satire the characters the  themes and what you thought about the  finale another season doesn't sound like  a bad idea in a different place with a  different cast i guess they will need to  come up with a way to make each season  feel fresh and unique but it looks like  they've got a good handle on things  please like this video if you enjoyed it  please subscribe to my channel if you  haven't already and thanks for watching  i'll talk to you soon

